; 27-infix.kurt

; Kurt's grammar is not fixed in Python -- you declare operators yourself,
; from Kurt source, and the parser (a Pratt/TDOP parser) picks them up right
; away.  `infix OP lbp rbp` declares `OP` as an infix operator with a left
; and a right binding power, which together decide precedence and
; associativity (`lbp > rbp` gives right-associativity, as `implies` already
; does in `minimal.kurt`: `infix implies 13 12` -- note `13 > 12`).

infix "+" 20 20        ; left-associative: binding powers are equal

var a, b
parse a + b            ; (+ a b)

; A newly declared symbol like `+` has no role yet, just grammar: it becomes
; a constant when it is first used in a fact (or a variable if you declare it
; with `var`, see 06-var.kurt) -- it carries no meaning yet.  Giving it
; meaning is a separate step, e.g. `load numbers` (see `src/kurt/theories/
; numbers.kurt`) for real arithmetic, or your own `use` axioms.
